From 000ed85e4e48771eff460bf4fc721fb43de80e08 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mike Bayer <mike_mp@zzzcomputing.com>
Date: Tue, 22 Sep 2026 15:53:38 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Fix path traversal via drive designator URI on Windows in
 TemplateLookup

The directory traversal check in Template.__init__ normalized the URI
using os.path, which on Windows is ntpath, while TemplateLookup.
get_template() resolves the URI to a file using posixpath.  The two
modules disagree about a drive designator: posixpath has no notion of
one and treats "C:" as an ordinary path component, so the ".." segments
of a URI such as "C:/../../secret.txt" escape the template root, while
ntpath splits the drive off, treats the remainder as rooted and absorbs
those same ".." segments, leaving nothing for the check to inspect.
The check therefore could not fire for any URI beginning with a drive
designator, and arbitrary readable files outside of the template
directory could be loaded.

Normalization in Template.__init__ is now performed with posixpath, the
same module get_template() already commits to, so that both sides of
the operation agree on what the URI means.

This is a third instance of the same posixpath/os.path mismatch behind
the fixes for #434 and #435; both of those remain effective on their
own terms and neither covers this form, as each normalized a separator
spelling rather than the drive designator.

The per-form traversal tests added for #434 and #435 are consolidated
into a single parametrized test in each of test_lookup.py and
test_template.py, running every spelling of the traversal against both
posixpath and ntpath.  All three forms are then covered by one matrix,
and reverting any one of the three fixes fails only the rows belonging
to it.

Fixes: #441
